<section id="introduction">
<h2>1. Introduction to Information Hiding</h2>

<p>
Information hiding is the process of concealing information inside
another digital file or communication. The information may be hidden
inside an image, PDF document, audio file, video or network traffic.
</p>

<h3>Steganography</h3>
<p>
Steganography is a technique used to hide the existence of information.
A person looking at the carrier file may not realise that another
message is contained inside it.
</p>

<h3>Cryptography</h3>
<p>
Cryptography protects information by transforming it into an
unreadable form. The correct key is normally needed to recover
the original message.
</p>

<h3>Steganalysis</h3>
<p>
Steganalysis is the investigation of files or communications to
identify possible hidden information.
</p>

<div class="note">
<strong>Important difference:</strong> Cryptography hides the meaning
of information, while steganography attempts to hide the existence
of the information itself.
</div>

</section>

<section>
<h2>2. Types of Steganography</h2>

<ul>
<li><strong>Pure steganography:</strong> hides information without
using a secret key.</li>
<li><strong>Private-key steganography:</strong> uses the same secret
key to hide and recover information.</li>
<li><strong>Public-key steganography:</strong> uses different but
related keys for hiding and recovering information.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Common carrier files</h3>
<ul>
<li>Image files: PNG, BMP, GIF and JPEG.</li>
<li>PDF documents.</li>
<li>Audio and video files.</li>
<li>Word documents and other digital files.</li>
</ul>

</section>

<section>
<h2>3. Caesar Cipher Practical Activity</h2>

<p>
A Caesar cipher is a substitution technique. It changes letters
by shifting them through the alphabet.
</p>

<p><strong>Week 5 encrypted message:</strong></p>
<p><code>Gr qrw jhw vfduhg ri wkh xqnrzq brx fdq dozdbv ohduq</code></p>

<p>
Using CyberChef with the Caesar cipher and shift 23 gives the
intended message:
</p>

<p><strong>Decoded message:</strong></p>
<p><code>Do not get scared of the unknown you can always learn</code></p>

<p>
This activity helped me understand how a simple substitution cipher
can be used to transform a message and how the correct shift can
recover the original text.
</p>

<a class="tool-link"
href="https://gchq.github.io/CyberChef/"
target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open CyberChef</a>

</section>

<section>
<h2>4. Base64 Encoding Practical Activity</h2>

<p>
Base64 converts data into a text representation using a set of
64 characters. It is commonly used to represent binary data in
text-based systems.
</p>

<p><strong>Original message:</strong></p>
<p><code>CLOUD LAB SAFE</code></p>

<p><strong>Base64 encoded message:</strong></p>
<p><code>Q0xPVUQgTEFCIFNBRkU=</code></p>

<div class="warning">
Base64 is encoding, not encryption. It does not require a secret
key and does not provide confidentiality. Anyone can decode it.
</div>

</section>

<section>
<h2>5. Image Steganography</h2>

<p>
Image steganography hides information inside an image. A digital
image consists of pixels, and each pixel can contain colour
components such as red, green and blue (RGB).
</p>

<h3>Least Significant Bit (LSB)</h3>

<p>
LSB means Least Significant Bit. It is a technique that stores
information by changing the lowest-value bit in a pixel colour
component. These small changes may be difficult to notice by
looking at the image.
</p>

<h3>Image formats</h3>

<table>
<tr>
<th>Format</th>
<th>Characteristics</th>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>BMP</td>
<td>Often uncompressed, allowing direct access to pixel data.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>GIF</td>
<td>Uses indexed colours and supports up to 256 colours in a frame.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>JPEG</td>
<td>Uses lossy compression, which can affect hidden information.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>PNG</td>
<td>Uses lossless compression and can preserve pixel values.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>Three important measurements</h3>

<ul>
<li><strong>Capacity:</strong> how much information can be hidden
inside an image.</li>
<li><strong>Detectability:</strong> how easily hidden information
can be discovered.</li>
<li><strong>Distortion:</strong> how much the image changes when
information is hidden.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Practical experiment</h3>

<p>
The Week 5 activity involved creating a harmless cover image using
a white background, blue rectangle and yellow circle. A test message
was then hidden inside the PNG image and recovered using a
steganography tool.
</p>

<p><strong>Test message:</strong></p>
<p><code>CC6004 | Training data only | Cloud transfer simulation</code></p>

<p>
The original cover image and the image containing the hidden
message can be compared. The message should be recoverable from
the stego image.
</p>

<a class="tool-link"
href="https://stylesuxx.github.io/steganography/"
target="_blank" rel="noopener">Image Steganography Tool</a>

<div class="note">
PNG uses lossless compression. JPEG uses lossy compression,
which may damage hidden data in some basic steganography methods.
</div>

</section>

<section>
<h2>6. DCT, Quantisation and PSNR</h2>

<h3>Discrete Cosine Transform (DCT)</h3>

<p>
The research paper explains that JPEG uses the Discrete Cosine
Transform to represent image information in frequency components.
The described example uses 8 × 8 pixel blocks.
</p>

<h3>Quantisation</h3>

<p>
Quantisation reduces or rounds values during compression.
This helps reduce file size but can also remove image information.
</p>

<h3>Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio (PSNR)</h3>

<p>
PSNR is a measurement used to compare image quality. It is
expressed in decibels (dB).
</p>

<p>
The paper reports the following values in its comparison:
</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Image format</th><th>Reported PSNR</th></tr>
<tr><td>PNG</td><td>9.34 dB</td></tr>
<tr><td>GIF</td><td>10.86 dB</td></tr>
<tr><td>BMP</td><td>65 dB in the table</td></tr>
<tr><td>JPEG</td><td>8.99 dB</td></tr>
</table>

<div class="warning">
The paper's table reports 65 dB for BMP, while its conclusion
mentions 68 dB. This is a discrepancy in the supplied paper.
These figures are author-reported experimental results.
</div>

</section>

<section>
<h2>7. Distributed Image Steganography: DSoBMP-I</h2>

<p>
The research paper describes a technique called DSoBMP-I.
It distributes hidden information across multiple images.
</p>

<p>
In the example, the original image is split into four parts
using a 2 × 2 arrangement. The parts are converted into BMP
images, and hidden information is distributed across them.
</p>

<p>
The authors report that their method increased capacity and
reduced detectability and distortion in their experiment.
</p>

<div class="note">
These results relate to the authors' particular experimental
setup. They should not be treated as universal performance
guarantees.
</div>

</section>

<section>
<h2>8. PDF Steganography</h2>

<p>
PDF documents can contain more than the text and images that
appear on the visible page. They may contain objects, metadata,
attachments, scripts and other structures.
</p>

<h3>PDF code investigation</h3>

<p>
The Week 5 PDF exercise included a visible sentence:
</p>

<p><code>This PDF I created from Scratch</code></p>

<p>
The example also contained additional text outside the visible
page content. This demonstrated why an investigator should not
only look at what appears on the PDF page.
</p>

<h3>Important PDF instructions</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Instruction</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td><code>BT</code></td><td>Begins a text object.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>/F1 18 Tf</code></td><td>Selects a font and font size.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>0 0 Td</code></td><td>Sets a text position.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>Tj</code></td><td>Displays text.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>ET</code></td><td>Ends a text object.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>xref</code></td><td>Introduces the cross-reference table.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>trailer</code></td><td>Contains PDF document information.</td></tr>
<tr><td><code>%%EOF</code></td><td>Expected end-of-file marker.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>PDF investigation methods</h3>

<ul>
<li>Text and spacing analysis.</li>
<li>JavaScript investigation.</li>
<li>Metadata analysis.</li>
<li>Structural analysis.</li>
<li>Statistical analysis.</li>
<li>Dynamic analysis.</li>
<li>Checking attachments and embedded objects.</li>
</ul>

<div class="warning">
Extra text outside visible page content does not automatically
prove malware or a valid hidden message. PDF structure and
parser behaviour must be examined.
</div>

</section>

<section>
<h2>9. Statistical Analysis</h2>

<p>
Statistical analysis uses mathematical methods to investigate
patterns in files and identify possible hidden information.
</p>

<p>
The lecture discusses mathematical algorithms, pattern
recognition, neural networks and genetic algorithms as possible
approaches.
</p>

<p>
In image steganography, statistical analysis may identify
unusual patterns caused by substitution techniques such as LSB.
</p>

</section>

<section>
<h2>10. Dynamic Analysis</h2>

<p>
Dynamic analysis investigates how a file or program behaves
during examination or execution in a controlled environment.
</p>

<p>
It can help identify suspicious activity that may not be
obvious from the file's visible content.
</p>

<p>
The lecture discusses intelligent software, machine learning,
digital signatures, insecure loading and component usage.
</p>

</section>

<section>
<h2>11. Metadata Analysis</h2>

<p>
Metadata is information that describes a digital file. It may
include file properties, creation information, modification
details and other technical information.
</p>

<p>
Investigators can examine metadata to identify unusual or
inconsistent details.
</p>

<div class="note">
Metadata is not always the same as a file header. Different
file formats store metadata in different ways.
</div>

</section>

<section>
<h2>12. Compression and Information Hiding</h2>

<p>
Compression reduces the size of digital files. It is commonly
used when storing or transferring large documents and
multimedia files.
</p>

<p>
The lecture discusses compression in PDF documents and the
possibility of combining compression with other hiding methods.
</p>

<p>
Different compression methods and file structures may affect
how security tools inspect files.
</p>

</section>

<section>
<h2>13. Network Steganography</h2>

<p>
Network steganography hides information inside network
communication. One example discussed in the Week 5 activities
is hiding encoded data inside DNS queries.
</p>

<h3>Example observations</h3>

<table>
<tr><th>Observation</th><th>Possible interpretation</th></tr>
<tr>
<td>Four ordinary DNS queries</td>
<td>May be normal network activity.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>48 long and varied DNS queries in two minutes</td>
<td>Suspicious pattern that needs investigation.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Large HTTPS upload to an unapproved destination</td>
<td>Possible unauthorised data transfer.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Scheduled encrypted backup to approved storage</td>
<td>May be expected if it matches the approved process.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>DNS and HTTPS investigation</h3>

<p>
DNS queries can contain encoded information in subdomain labels.
Unusual query length and frequency may be indicators of possible
DNS tunnelling or concealed communication.
</p>

<p>
HTTPS encrypts the content travelling between systems, but
metadata such as destination, timing and traffic volume may
still be observable.
</p>

<div class="warning">
Unusual traffic is an indicator, not proof of data theft.
Investigators should compare DNS logs, endpoint activity,
proxy records and cloud audit logs.
</div>

</section>

<section>
<h2>14. Cloud Security and Defence in Depth</h2>

<p>
Cloud services allow users to store and share information
through internet-based platforms. Information hiding can
create risks when data is transferred without permission.
</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Security risk</th><th>Possible defence</th></tr>
<tr>
<td>Anonymous sharing link exposes restricted file</td>
<td>Private sharing and least privilege.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Sensitive data copied to USB</td>
<td>Endpoint Data Loss Prevention.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Unapproved external cloud upload</td>
<td>Cloud DLP, CASB and egress controls.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Suspicious DNS activity</td>
<td>DNS monitoring and investigation.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>File changed without approval</td>
<td>Versioning and audit logs.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Licensed diagram reposted without credit</td>
<td>Watermarking and appropriate rights management.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<h3>Important cloud security terms</h3>

<ul>
<li><strong>DLP:</strong> Data Loss Prevention, which helps protect
sensitive information from inappropriate sharing or transfer.</li>
<li><strong>CASB:</strong> Cloud Access Security Broker, which
helps monitor and enforce policies for cloud applications.</li>
<li><strong>EDR:</strong> Endpoint Detection and Response, which
helps investigate and respond to endpoint threats.</li>
<li><strong>Least privilege:</strong> users receive only the
permissions they need.</li>
<li><strong>Defence in depth:</strong> multiple security controls
are used together rather than relying on one control.</li>
</ul>

</section>

<section>
<h2>15. Cloud Incident Investigation</h2>

<p>
The Week 5 fictional incident log showed a sequence of
suspicious cloud activities:
</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Time</th><th>Event</th></tr>
<tr><td>09:00</td><td>Known device signs in.</td></tr>
<tr><td>09:02</td><td>Unfamiliar browser session appears.</td></tr>
<tr><td>09:04</td><td>research-plan.png is downloaded.</td></tr>
<tr><td>09:06</td><td>Sharing link changed to anyone with the link.</td></tr>
<tr><td>09:08</td><td>85 MB uploaded to unapproved external storage.</td></tr>
<tr><td>09:09</td><td>DLP alert identifies restricted research label.</td></tr>
<tr><td>09:11</td><td>Administrator revokes the sharing link.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Investigation priorities</h3>

<ol>
<li>Investigate the unfamiliar browser session.</li>
<li>Review the public sharing link.</li>
<li>Investigate the 85 MB external upload.</li>
<li>Review the DLP alert and file classification.</li>
<li>Preserve relevant logs and evidence.</li>
<li>Revoke risky access and review active sessions.</li>
</ol>

<div class="warning">
Revoking a sharing link does not undo downloads that may
have happened before the link was revoked.
</div>

</section>

<section>
<h2>16. Hashing and File Integrity</h2>

<p>
A cryptographic hash creates a fixed-length value from digital
data. SHA-256 is an example of a cryptographic hash function.
</p>

<p><strong>Original test message:</strong></p>
<p><code>CC6004 approved cloud research file</code></p>

<p>
Adding a full stop changes the message and produces a different
SHA-256 hash.
</p>

<h3>What hashing can tell us</h3>

<ul>
<li>It can help identify whether file contents have changed.</li>
<li>Matching hashes provide strong practical evidence that
two files contain the same bytes.</li>
<li>A hash does not prove who created a file.</li>
<li>A hash does not prove that a file is safe or truthful.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Additional cloud protections</h3>

<ul>
<li>Object versioning.</li>
<li>Immutable audit logs.</li>
<li>Retention controls.</li>
<li>Access control and monitoring.</li>
</ul>

</section>

<section>
<h2>17. VirusTotal and EICAR Practical Activity</h2>

<p>
VirusTotal is an online service that can be used to examine
file hashes and review security detection information.
</p>

<p>
The Week 5 activity instructed students to search for the
EICAR test string hash rather than upload files.
</p>

<p><strong>SHA-256 hash:</strong></p>

<p>
<code>275a021bbfb6489e54d471899f7db9d1663fc695ec2fe2a2c4538aabf651fd0f</code>
</p>

<a class="tool-link"
href="https://www.virustotal.com/"
target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open VirusTotal</a>

<div class="warning">
EICAR is a harmless antivirus test signature. An antivirus
