has occurred. Detection counts can change over time.
</div>

</section>

<section>
<h2>18. Fictional Malware Alert Investigation</h2>

<p>
The Week 5 fictional malware alert contained the following events:
</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Time</th><th>Event</th></tr>
<tr><td>10:00</td><td>External guest uploads invoice_Q4.zip.</td></tr>
<tr><td>10:01</td><td>Scanner flags the file.</td></tr>
<tr><td>10:02</td><td>File shared with 18 staff members.</td></tr>
<tr><td>10:05</td><td>Two people preview the file.</td></tr>
<tr><td>10:07</td><td>One endpoint downloads the file.</td></tr>
<tr><td>10:09</td><td>Endpoint protection blocks an attempted launch.</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Recommended response</h3>

<ol>
<li>Restrict or quarantine the suspicious file.</li>
<li>Preserve cloud and endpoint logs.</li>
<li>Investigate who accessed or downloaded the file.</li>
<li>Review the endpoint protection alert.</li>
<li>Check the file hash and scanner results.</li>
<li>Confirm the activity with the relevant user or administrator.</li>
</ol>

<div class="note">
A preview or download does not prove malware executed.
A blocked launch does not prove that execution succeeded.
</div>

</section>

<section>
