<h3>Important security tools</h3>

<ul>
<li>Cloud-native malware scanning checks uploaded files.</li>
<li>CASB monitors and enforces cloud application policies.</li>
<li>DLP helps protect sensitive information.</li>
<li>EDR investigates and responds to endpoint threats.</li>
</ul>

<p>
Encrypted ZIP files and images containing hidden information
may not be fully assessed by basic scanning tools.
</p>

</section>

<section>
<h2>20. Social Engineering</h2>

<p>
Social engineering is the use of manipulation to persuade
people to reveal information, click links or perform actions
that may compromise security.
</p>

<h3>Common techniques</h3>

<ul>
<li><strong>Urgency:</strong> pressuring someone to act quickly.</li>
<li><strong>Authority:</strong> pretending to be a manager or
trusted person.</li>
<li><strong>Fear:</strong> threatening account closure or other
negative consequences.</li>
<li><strong>Curiosity:</strong> encouraging someone to open an
unexpected attachment.</li>
<li><strong>Trust:</strong> pretending to represent a familiar
organisation.</li>
</ul>

<h3>How to reduce the risk</h3>

<ul>
<li>Check unexpected emails and messages carefully.</li>
<li>Verify unusual requests through a trusted channel.</li>
<li>Never share passwords or authentication codes.</li>
<li>Use multi-factor authentication (MFA).</li>
<li>Report suspicious messages.</li>
</ul>

<div class="note">
The lecture identifies people as an important part of
cybersecurity. Security awareness and technical controls
should work together.
</div>

</section>

<section>
<h2>21. Deepfakes and Media Provenance</h2>

<p>
A deepfake is media that has been generated or manipulated
to look or sound like a real person or event.
</p>

<p>
Media provenance is information about where media came from
and how it has been handled or changed.
</p>

<h3>Investigating suspicious media</h3>

<ul>
<li>Find the original source.</li>
<li>Check available timestamps and upload history.</li>
<li>Look for independent confirmation.</li>
<li>Check official communication channels.</li>
<li>Look for signed provenance information where available.</li>
<li>Do not treat a video alone as proof of a claim.</li>
</ul>

<div class="warning">
A provenance record can help establish the history of media,
but it does not automatically prove that the statements in
the media are true.
</div>

</section>

<section>
<h2>22. Detection Metrics</h2>

<p>
Detection systems can be evaluated using four measures:
true positives, false positives, false negatives and
true negatives.
</p>

<table>
<tr><th>Measure</th><th>Value</th></tr>
<tr><td>True positives (TP)</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>False positives (FP)</td><td>2</td></tr>
<tr><td>False negatives (FN)</td><td>12</td></tr>
<tr><td>True negatives (TN)</td><td>78</td></tr>
<tr><td>Total</td><td>100</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Precision</h3>
<p>
Precision = TP ÷ (TP + FP)
</p>
<p>
8 ÷ (8 + 2) = <strong>80%</strong>
</p>

<h3>Recall</h3>
<p>
Recall = TP ÷ (TP + FN)
</p>
<p>
8 ÷ (8 + 12) = <strong>40%</strong>
</p>

<h3>F1 Score</h3>
<p>
F1 score = 2 × (Precision × Recall) ÷ (Precision + Recall)
</p>
<p><strong>Approximately 53.3%</strong></p>

<div class="note">
The system has 80% precision but only 40% recall. It correctly
identifies many of its alerts, but it misses 12 of the 20
actual positive cases. This shows why recall is important
when evaluating security detection.
</div>

</section>

